{"tag":"VHOU","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"theorem-1","file":"","label":"thm:main","number":"2.1","section":"sec-2","order":1,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:main}\n\tFor every prime $p\\geq3$,\n\t\\[\n\tH_{p,1/3}\\equiv a_p(f_{12.4.a.a})\\pmod{p^3},\n\t\\]\n\tand\n\t\\[\n\tH_{p,1/6}\\equiv a_p(f_{24.4.a.a})\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:main"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":154,"to":163},"hash":"sha256:4ee7d3e0077cec4cd5b9cef1b0535fce290de48d3211f28f42e629c248232857","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"EM0X","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"lemma-1","file":"","label":"lem:116-initial","number":"3.1","section":"sec-4","order":2,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:116-initial}\n\tFor $0\\leq k\\leq N$, one has\n\t\\[\n\tv_p(b_{1/6,k})\\geq0.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:116-initial"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":339,"to":344},"hash":"sha256:2d84ac5c40867bc6c3f527577136869cc981aab4970c8804f36689a5c6f58e9c","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"ZBPV","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-4","order":3,"title":"Proof","body_md":"For $k\u003cp$, the factor $(k!)^4$ is a $p$-adic unit.  Each rational\n\tPochhammer symbol displayed above contains at most one factor divisible\n\tby $p$, since the corresponding linear congruence has a unique solution\n\tmodulo $p$.  After clearing the denominators $2$, $3$, and $6$, every\n\trelevant positive linear factor is at most $6p-5$.  Since\n\t\\[\n\t6p-5\u003cp^2\n\t\\qquad (p\\geq7),\n\t\\]\n\teach such $p$-divisible factor has valuation exactly one.\n\t\n\tThe first crossing thresholds are\n\t\\[\n\t\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\n\t\t\u0026(\\frac12)_k\u0026(\\frac16)_k\u0026(\\frac56)_k\n\t\t\u0026(\\frac43)_k\u0026(\\frac23)_k\\\\ \\hline\n\t\tp=6m+1\u00263m+1\u0026m+1\u00265m+1\u00262m\u00264m+1\\\\\n\t\tp=6m+5\u00263m+3\u00265m+5\u0026m+1\u00264m+3\u00262m+2\n\t\\end{array}\n\t\\]\n\t\n\tIf $p=6m+1$, then $N=3m$.  The denominator factor\n\t$(\\frac43)_k$ first acquires a $p$-factor at $k=2m$, but\n\t$(\\frac16)_k$ has already acquired one at $k=m+1$; moreover,\n\t$(\\frac23)_k$ does not cross before $N$.\n\t\n\tIf $p=6m+5$, then $N=3m+2$.  The denominator factor\n\t$(\\frac23)_k$ first acquires a $p$-factor at $k=2m+2$, but\n\t$(\\frac56)_k$ has already acquired one at $k=m+1$; moreover,\n\t$(\\frac43)_k$ does not cross before $N$.\n\t\n\tThe remaining factor $4k+1$ lies in the numerator and therefore cannot\n\tdecrease the valuation.  Thus the denominator never contributes greater\n\t$p$-adic valuation than the numerator, and hence\n\t\\[\n\tv_p(b_{1/6,k})\\geq0.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":346,"to":384},"hash":"sha256:c5da29713995ffb60a0e781b9a2f74ab41429f59ad4704d3284165442dce3e43","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"99F1","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"lemma-2","file":"","label":"lem:C-symmetry","number":"3.2","section":"sec-5","order":4,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:C-symmetry}\n\tFor $k\\in I_p$,\n\t\\[\n\tC_{N-k}\\equiv C_k\\pmod p.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:C-symmetry"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":482,"to":487},"hash":"sha256:2f9f5ab68b1d11af289a75d98d595bb6063c7dc562dc6f5f60bf47624813878e","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"M31K","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":5,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Put\n\t\\[\n\tc_j:=\n\t\\frac{1}{(j+\\frac12)^3}\n\t+\n\t\\frac{1}{(j+1)^3},\n\t\\qquad\n\tC_k=\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}c_j.\n\t\\]\n\tSince $N\\equiv-\\frac12\\pmod p$,\n\t\\[\n\tN-\\frac12-j\\equiv-(j+1)\\pmod p,\n\t\\qquad\n\tN-j\\equiv-\\left(j+\\frac12\\right)\\pmod p,\n\t\\]\n\tand hence\n\t\\[\n\tc_{N-1-j}\\equiv-c_j\\pmod p.\n\t\\]\n\tIt follows that $C_N\\equiv0\\pmod p$.  Moreover,\n\t\\[\n\tC_N-C_{N-k}\n\t=\n\t\\sum_{j=N-k}^{N-1}c_j\n\t\\equiv\n\t-\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}c_j\n\t=\n\t-C_k\n\t\\pmod p,\n\t\\]\n\twhich gives the claim.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":489,"to":521},"hash":"sha256:caa22b47e7dcc92ae3f7dce959b389be504658d7ced83868c3fef9e0df2c4470","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"JW6Z","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"lemma-3","file":"","label":"lem:q-antisymmetry","number":"3.3","section":"sec-5","order":6,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:q-antisymmetry}\n\tFor $k\\in I_p$,\n\t\\[\n\tq_{N-k}\\equiv-q_k\\pmod p.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:q-antisymmetry"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":523,"to":528},"hash":"sha256:b7c2271bfa6aa8ffc978b66c1121c19f7bd0ba438751c18b57c057337a93b1d4","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"KVSU","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proof-3","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":7,"title":"Proof","body_md":"The ratio of consecutive terms is\n\t\\[\n\tr_k:=\n\t\\frac{b_{1/3,k+1}}{b_{1/3,k}}\n\t=\n\t\\frac{4k+5}{4k+1}\n\t\\frac{(k+\\frac12)^4(k+\\frac13)(k+\\frac23)}\n\t{(k+1)^4(k+\\frac56)(k+\\frac76)}.\n\t\\]\n\tAs a rational function of $z$,\n\t\\[\n\tr(z)\\,r\\left(-z-\\frac32\\right)=1.\n\t\\]\n\tSince\n\t\\[\n\tN-k-1\\equiv-k-\\frac32\\pmod p,\n\t\\]\n\twe obtain\n\t\\[\n\tr_kr_{N-k-1}\\equiv1\\pmod p\n\t\\]\n\taway from the possible central zero or pole.  Thus\n\t$q_{N-k}/q_k$ is constant as one moves outward through paired indices.\n\t\n\tIf $N$ is odd, let $c=(N-1)/2$.  Then $N-c=c+1$ and\n\t$c\\equiv-3/4\\pmod p$, so direct substitution gives\n\t\\[\n\tr_c\\equiv-1\\pmod p.\n\t\\]\n\tIf $N$ is even, let $c=N/2=(p-1)/4$.  Then $q_c=0$, and after\n\tcancelling the central zero and pole one obtains\n\t\\[\n\tr_{c-1}r_c\\equiv-1\\pmod p.\n\t\\]\n\tIn either case the sign $-1$ propagates through all paired indices,\n\twhich proves the result.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-3","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":530,"to":567},"hash":"sha256:e582dc227c976bc9ad3119ce52ca709aae9ca763d40fe8e0fef4f17a661a6e47","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#proof-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"9QLJ","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proposition-1","file":"","label":"prop:initial-bridge","number":"3.4","section":"sec-5","order":8,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{prop:initial-bridge}\n\tFor $\\rho\\in\\{\\frac13,\\frac16\\}$, one has\n\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:initial-bridge}\n\t\tH_{p,\\rho}\\equiv H_\\rho(-p/2)\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proposition","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["prop:initial-bridge","eq:initial-bridge"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":624,"to":629},"hash":"sha256:10ffb7f95ebdc0777ab39e65a88eeab540f5d0ea5087b26858ef97a06573c1d8","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"NAD4","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"lemma-4","file":"","label":"lem:cubic-collapse","number":"7.1","section":"sec-9","order":9,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lem:cubic-collapse}\n\tFor $\\rho\\in\\{\\frac13,\\frac16\\}$, one has\n\t\\[\n\tJ_\\rho\\equiv U_\\rho\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["lem:cubic-collapse"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":1125,"to":1130},"hash":"sha256:f30db08fb198d7510cbb8610b885d3fea7336b2c8e0de7faad8a9d58caabc676","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"GR91","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proposition-2","file":"","label":"prop:common-shift","number":"9.1","section":"sec-11","order":10,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{prop:common-shift}\n\tFor $\\rho\\in\\{\\frac13,\\frac16\\}$ and every $s\\in\\mathbb{Z}_p$,\n\t\\[\n\t\\mathcal G_\\rho(s)\\equiv\\mathcal G_\\rho(0)\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proposition","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","name":"Normalized common-shift invariance","labels":["prop:common-shift"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":1261,"to":1267},"hash":"sha256:8b70c8a36044cac756689fa081f201e4bad9bac45e8bb6989073f27d0f82bc58","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"TLOB","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proof-4","file":"","section":"sec-11","order":11,"title":"Proof","body_md":"The main perturbation calculation is precisely the one carried out by\n\tLong--Tu--Yui--Zudilin \\cite[Lemma~5 and its proof]{MR4330088}.\n\tWe record only the specialization needed here.\n\t\nWith $\\alpha_\\rho=\\{r_1,r_2,r_3,r_4\\}$ as above, set\n\t\\[\n\tA_k:=\\frac{\\prod_{j=1}^4(r_j)_k}{(k!)^4},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\gamma_\\rho:=\\prod_{j=1}^4\\Gamma_p(r_j).\n\t\\]\n\t\n\tFor each $r_j$, let $[-r_j]_0\\in\\{0,1,\\ldots,p-1\\}$ denote the\n\tleast nonnegative residue of $-r_j$ modulo $p$.  Then\n\t$r_j+[-r_j]_0$ is divisible by $p$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_p$, so we may write\n\t\\[\n\tr_j+[-r_j]_0=pr_j',\n\t\\qquad r_j'\\in\\mathbb{Z}_p.\n\t\\]\n\t\n\tDefine\n\t\\[\n\t\\Lambda_{\\rho,k}(s)\n\t:=\n\t\\prod_{j=1}^4\n\t\\left(1+\\frac{s}{r_j'}\\right)^{\\nu(k,[-r_j]_0)},\n\t\\]\n\twhere $\\nu(k,a)=0$ for $k\\leq a$ and $\\nu(k,a)=1$ for $k\u003ea$.\n\t\n\tSpecializing the $p$-adic perturbation expansion of\n\tLong--Tu--Yui--Zudilin to the datum $\\alpha_\\rho$ gives\n\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:LTYZ-specialized}\n\t\t\\mathcal G_\\rho(s)-\\mathcal G_\\rho(0)\n\t\t\\equiv\n\t\t\\gamma_\\rho\n\t\t\\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\n\t\tA_k\n\t\t\\left[\n\t\t\\Lambda_{\\rho,k}(s)\n\t\t\\bigl(1+J_1(k)sp+J_2(k)(sp)^2\\bigr)-1\n\t\t\\right]\n\t\t\\pmod{p^3},\n\t\\end{equation}\n\twhere $J_1(k)$ and $J_2(k)$ are the coefficients arising from their\n\tlocal $p$-adic gamma expansion.\n\t\n\tFor $s=b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}$, the sum on the right-hand side of\n\t\\eqref{eq:LTYZ-specialized} is exactly the perturbation expression\n\ttreated in \\cite[Lemma~5]{MR4330088}; hence\n\t\\[\n\t\\mathcal G_\\rho(b)\\equiv\\mathcal G_\\rho(0)\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\]\n\t\n\tIt remains only to extend the parameter from\n\t$b\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}$ to $s\\in\\mathbb{Z}_p$.\n\tThe right-hand side of \\eqref{eq:LTYZ-specialized} is a finite\n\tcontinuous $p$-adic function of $s$.  Since the nonnegative integers\n\tare dense in $\\mathbb{Z}_p$ and $p^3\\mathbb{Z}_p$ is closed, the same congruence holds\n\tfor every $s\\in\\mathbb{Z}_p$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"proposition-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":1269,"to":1328},"hash":"sha256:84c26d3b2ce94286a3dc4caae5c4b2410617523f5defec26a59b81694cd2f145","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#proof-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"TW7Q","kind":"remark","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"remark-1","file":"","number":"9.2","section":"sec-11","order":12,"title":"Remark","body_md":"Only the specialization $s=-\\frac12$ is needed below.  Proposition\n\t\\ref{prop:common-shift} is stated in its natural $p$-adic form because\n\tthe same family may have other useful terminating or resonant\n\tspecializations.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"remark","item":"remark","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":1331,"to":1336},"hash":"sha256:e38170b5740dd1d3beb65779393d5cf1e09ad3f1b4571f9eec59becbdf2ebbcc","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"O56V","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.15176","version":"1","local":"proposition-3","file":"","label":"prop:common-final-bridge","number":"10.1","section":"sec-14","order":13,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{prop:common-final-bridge}\n\tFor $\\rho\\in\\left\\{\\frac13,\\frac16\\right\\}$,\n\tone has\n\t\\[\n\tR_\\rho c_N^2U_\\rho\n\t\\equiv\n\t\\left(\\frac{-3}{p}\\right)S_{p,\\rho}\n\t\\pmod{p^3}.\n\t\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proposition","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["prop:common-final-bridge"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Whipple_two_supercongruences.tex","from":1641,"to":1650},"hash":"sha256:3bb565d23e1ca0a7d83360545bf2ffcdb33ca4db2bf0af4681c441a8ed673feb","minhash":"","census":"2610.15176v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
